<h2 id="zettelkasten">What is a Zettelkasten?</h2>

<p>A <em>Zettelkasten</em> ("slip box" in German) is a note-taking method developed by the German sociologist <strong>Niklas Luhmann</strong>, who used it across forty years of work to produce around seventy books and four hundred peer-reviewed papers. His Zettelkasten contained roughly 90,000 paper slips. When asked how he was so prolific, Luhmann famously replied that he wasn't — his Zettelkasten was.</p>

<p>The method has four central principles, all of which this app is built around:</p>

<p><strong>1. Atomic notes.</strong> Each note expresses one idea, one observation, or one argument — not a topic, not a chapter, not a meeting summary. The unit is the smallest thing that can stand on its own. A note titled "Algorithmic governance erodes accountability" is atomic; a note titled "Notes on Aneesh's chapter 3" is not. The reason for atomicity is that small ideas combine and recombine in ways that large ones cannot — an atomic note about dissonance can show up unexpectedly in arguments about labor, surveillance, or affect, whereas a chapter summary can only be cited as itself.</p>

<p><strong>2. Links between notes are the structure.</strong> A Zettelkasten has no folder hierarchy. Notes are connected to each other by direct references — in this app, that's the <code>[[id]]</code> wikilink syntax. Over time the web of links becomes the structure: it grows organically from your actual thinking rather than from a category scheme you imposed in advance. The <a href="#wikilinks">backlinks panel</a> on every note shows what other notes have referenced it, which is how new connections surface.</p>

<p><strong>3. Notes are written in your own words.</strong> A Zettelkasten is not a quote collection. The act of rewriting an idea in your own words, in your own voice, is the moment of understanding. If you copy a passage verbatim, you have moved text from one place to another but you have not yet thought about it. Sönke Ahrens makes this argument forcefully in <em>How to Take Smart Notes</em> (2017): the friction of rewriting <em>is</em> the cognitive work, and shortcuts that bypass it (including AI-generated summaries) defeat the epistemic point of the method.</p>

<p><strong>4. The system serves long-term writing, not retrieval.</strong> A Zettelkasten is not a search engine for your past readings — for that you have Google Scholar and Zotero. It is a thinking partner across years. You write notes today not because you need them today, but because some future project will surprise you by drawing on them. This is why the method rewards consistency more than volume.</p>

<p><strong>How this app maps onto the method.</strong> Wikilinks <code>[[id]]</code> are how you connect notes; backlinks (shown automatically on every note view) are how connections surface in reverse. Tags are a lightweight grouping mechanism for cross-cutting themes that don't deserve their own thread. <a href="#threads">Threads</a> are this app's implementation of Luhmann's <em>Folgezettel</em> — sequential chains where notes follow each other in a deliberate argumentative order, and a single note can belong to multiple threads simultaneously. The <a href="#cite">citation system</a> lets you reference Zotero entries by <code>@citekey</code> without copying their text — your notes stay in your voice, while pointing precisely at the sources behind your claims.</p>

<p>For deeper reading: Sönke Ahrens, <em>How to Take Smart Notes</em> (2nd ed., 2022); Luhmann's own brief essay <em>Communicating with Slip Boxes</em> (1981, translated by Manfred Kuehn); and the writing of Andy Matuschak on evergreen notes at <code>notes.andymatuschak.org</code>.</p>

<h2 id="basics">The basics</h2>

<p>Every note is a single plain Markdown file in your <code>notes/</code> folder. The web interface you are reading right now is one way to interact with those files; any text editor you like is another. The two are kept in sync automatically — edit a file in Obsidian, vim, or TextEdit and the index will update within a second or two.</p>

<p>Notes have a 14-digit timestamp ID (like <code>20260407164313</code>) and a slug derived from the title. The ID is forever; the title can change without breaking anything that links to the note.</p>

<p><strong>To create a note:</strong> click <a href="{{ url_for('new') }}">+ new</a> in the top right. Give it a title and a body in Markdown. Save.</p>

<p><strong>To edit a note:</strong> open it and click <em>edit</em> at the bottom.</p>

<p><strong>To delete a note:</strong> open it and click the red <em>delete</em> button. See <a href="#delete">Deleting &amp; trash</a> below for what actually happens.</p>

<h2 id="kinds">Kinds — four streams for different purposes</h2>

<p>Zetteling organizes notes along a <em>kind</em> axis with four values: <strong>research</strong>, <strong>journal</strong>, <strong>teaching</strong>, and <strong>life</strong>. Every note belongs to exactly one kind. Research is the default; existing notes created before v3.14 are treated as research automatically.</p>

<p>The kinds are streams for different purposes, not workflow stages. Nothing processes a note from one kind to another — a journal entry doesn't graduate into a research note, they're categorically different. The point is to keep journal entries out of your research indexes without forcing you to run a separate app.</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Research</strong> notes use the full Zettelkasten machinery: tags, threads (folgezettel chains), bibliographic refs, wikilinks, backlinks, semantic related-notes. The thread fieldset on the new-note form is only shown for research notes because folgezettel sequences don't apply to daily-journal entries or teaching plans.</li>
  <li><strong>Journal</strong> entries are for day-level journaling — interstitial notes through a day, end-of-day reflections, or anything else date-anchored. The journal index has a <em>today's entry</em> button that creates or resumes a journal note titled with today's date (YYYY-MM-DD). Click it once in the morning, fill it in through the day; click it again and you're back in the same note.</li>
  <li><strong>Teaching</strong> notes are for syllabi, lecture plans, student-facing materials, course reflections. You can still use refs (to cite assigned readings) and tags (per course or topic). Threads are available if you need them, but default-hidden on new notes.</li>
  <li><strong>Life</strong> notes are for everything else — academic-life reflections, administrative notes, personal observations, correspondence drafts. Lightweight by default, no required structure.</li>
</ul>

<p>The navigation bar at the top lets you jump to each kind's filtered index. The index page also has tab controls so you can switch kinds without leaving the page, including an <em>all</em> view that shows everything mixed. In the <em>all</em> view, and in cross-kind contexts like the semantic related-notes panel or backlinks, each note is prefixed with a small colored dot indicating its kind so you can tell at a glance which stream it came from.</p>

<p><strong>Cross-kind links work freely.</strong> A research note can link to a teaching note can link to a journal entry via wikilinks. Backlinks span kinds. Full-text search spans kinds. Semantic find-related-notes spans kinds — one of the most valuable use cases is a research note surfacing a journal entry from six months ago that turned out to be related. The kinds segregate the <em>indexes</em>, not the <em>corpus</em>.</p>

<p>You can change a note's kind later by editing it — the kind selector appears at the top of the edit form, and changing it updates the frontmatter on save.</p>

<h2 id="wikilinks">Wikilinks</h2>

<p>To link from one note to another, type the destination's 14-digit ID in double square brackets:</p>

<pre><code>This idea builds on [[20260407164313]] and extends it.</code></pre>

<p>The link will render as the destination note's title. To use custom link text instead, add a pipe and your alias:</p>

<pre><code>This builds on [[20260407164313|my earlier dissonance argument]].</code></pre>

<p>If the destination ID does not exist, the link renders in a dashed grey style — useful for marking "I want to write this note someday." Once you create a note with that ID, the link becomes live automatically.</p>

<p><strong>Backlinks happen for free.</strong> Every note's view page shows a "Backlinks" panel listing every other note that links <em>to</em> it. There is no manual backlink management.</p>

<h2 id="tags">Tags</h2>

<p>Add tags in the edit form as a comma-separated list. They become clickable chips on the note view, link to a tag page listing every note carrying that tag, and appear in the master index at <a href="{{ url_for('tags_index') }}">tags</a>.</p>

<p>Tag names should be lowercase, hyphenated, and consistent — <code>sts-theory</code>, not <code>STS Theory</code>. The system does not enforce this; it is a discipline thing.</p>

<h2 id="threads">Threads (folgezettel sequences)</h2>

<p>A <em>thread</em> is a named sequence of notes linked by prev/next pointers — useful when you are working out an argument across multiple atomic notes and want to be able to walk through them in order. Unlike tags, a single note can be a member of multiple threads simultaneously. Unlike wikilinks, threads impose a sequence rather than a graph.</p>

<p>Threads are currently configured by editing a note's YAML frontmatter directly. Open <code>notes/your-note.md</code> in any text editor and add a <code>threads:</code> block:</p>

<pre><code>---
id: '20260407164313'
title: Algorithmic dissonance, first move
threads:
  - name: dissonance-arc
    prev: null
    next: '20260407164314'
---</code></pre>

<p>Then on the next note in the sequence:</p>

<pre><code>---
id: '20260407164314'
title: Algorithmic dissonance, second move
threads:
  - name: dissonance-arc
    prev: '20260407164313'
    next: '20260407164315'
---</code></pre>

<p>Save the files. The web UI picks them up immediately. Visit <a href="{{ url_for('threads_index') }}">threads</a> to see all configured threads, and click any thread name to walk its chain in order. On any individual note page, you'll see a strip showing its prev/next neighbors within each thread it belongs to.</p>

<p>A graphical thread editor is on the roadmap but deliberately not built yet — I want to see whether the YAML approach is actually painful in real use first.</p>

<h2 id="search">Search</h2>

<p>The search box on the home page does a full-text search across the title, body, and tags of every note, with snippet highlighting and ranked results. It uses SQLite's FTS5 engine, which means you can use search operators:</p>

<ul>
  <li><code>drone</code> — matches notes containing "drone"</li>
  <li><code>drone NOT military</code> — drone, but exclude any with "military"</li>
  <li><code>drone OR uav</code> — either word</li>
  <li><code>"civilian drone"</code> — exact phrase</li>
  <li><code>drone*</code> — prefix match (drone, drones, droning)</li>
</ul>

<p>Search is case-insensitive and uses Porter stemming, so <em>orienting</em>, <em>oriented</em>, and <em>orientation</em> all match a search for <em>orient</em>.</p>

<h2 id="cite">Citations and Zotero integration</h2>

<p>If you have a bibliography configured (the <code>cite</code> link in the top nav will list entries; otherwise it shows a setup hint), you can reference works in any note using Pandoc-style citekeys:</p>

<pre><code>Building on @aneesh2009 and @browne2015, we can see…</code></pre>

<p>While editing, type <code>@</code> and a picker pops up in the bottom right of the screen. Type to filter by citekey, title, or author; arrow keys to navigate; Enter to insert. In the rendered note, citekeys become links with hover tooltips showing author, year, and title.</p>

<p>To set up the bibliography, in Zotero install the <a href="https://retorque.re/zotero-better-bibtex/">Better BibTeX</a> extension, configure it to auto-export your library to a file, then start the server with the path set:</p>

<pre><code>ZETTEL_BIB_PATH=/path/to/library.bib python app.py</code></pre>

<p>The server watches that file and re-indexes whenever Zotero rewrites it.</p>

<h2 id="related">Semantic find-related-notes</h2>

<p>If you've configured an embedding provider in <a href="{{ url_for('settings_page') }}">settings</a>, every note view shows a "Related (semantic)" sidebar with the most conceptually similar notes from your corpus, ranked by cosine distance. Smaller distance = more similar.</p>

<p>Each note gets embedded into a vector when you create or save it (best-effort — if the embedding call fails, the note still saves cleanly and you can backfill later via the reindex button in settings). The vectors live in a <code>sqlite-vec</code> table in the same database as the search index, and the related-notes panel does a vector nearest-neighbors query on every note view.</p>

<p>Three honest caveats to read these results well:</p>

<p><strong>1. Quality depends entirely on the embedding model.</strong> The default Ollama embedding model (<code>nomic-embed-text</code>) is decent but not state-of-the-art. OpenAI's <code>text-embedding-3-small</code> is meaningfully better. <code>text-embedding-3-large</code> is better still but more expensive. Google's <code>text-embedding-004</code> is competitive. If your first try with one model feels weak, switch in the settings page and click <em>Reindex all notes</em> — the embeddings will be recomputed against the new model and the related-notes results will change accordingly.</p>

<p><strong>2. The most useful results are usually the surprising ones.</strong> When you write a note about algorithmic dissonance and the panel surfaces a six-month-old note about algocracy that you'd forgotten existed, that's the system working as designed. When it surfaces three other notes from the same week that you obviously already remember, that's noise. Read the related notes as suggestions to investigate, not as a ranked truth — the value is in the unexpected ones.</p>

<p><strong>3. Distances are not normalized across models.</strong> A distance of 0.4 means something very different in <code>nomic-embed-text</code> than in <code>text-embedding-3-small</code>. Don't try to compare distances across reindexes with different models. The relative ranking <em>within</em> a single corpus on a single model is what matters — which note is closer than which other note — not the absolute numbers.</p>

<p>If you ever need to start fresh (you switched models, you imported a lot of new notes, or the index seems out of sync), use the <em>Reindex all notes</em> button on the settings page. It runs in the background with live progress and you can keep working while it happens.</p>

<h2 id="external">Editing notes outside the web UI</h2>

<p>This is the whole point of the plain-Markdown design: nothing locks you in. Open the <code>notes/</code> folder in Finder, edit any <code>.md</code> file in any editor — Obsidian, VS Code, vim, TextEdit, whatever — and save. The watcher picks up the change within a second or two and the index updates. The web UI will show your changes on the next page reload.</p>

<p>Two practical notes. First, do not put files in subdirectories of <code>notes/</code> — the watcher only looks at the top level. Second, if you rename a file's 14-digit ID prefix, you have effectively created a new note and orphaned all wikilinks pointing at the old ID. Don't do that. Edit the title, the body, the tags freely; leave the ID alone.</p>

<h2 id="delete">Deleting and the trash</h2>

<p>The delete button moves the note's file into <code>notes/.trash/</code> with a timestamp suffix appended to the filename. The note disappears from the index immediately. The file itself is still on your disk, recoverable.</p>

<p><strong>To restore a deleted note:</strong> open <code>notes/.trash/</code> in Finder, find the file (filenames look like <code>20260407164313-mynote.md.20260407164314.deleted</code>), drag it back into <code>notes/</code>, and rename it to remove the <code>.20260407164314.deleted</code> suffix so it ends in <code>.md</code> again. The watcher will pick it up.</p>

<p><strong>To permanently delete:</strong> empty <code>notes/.trash/</code> in Finder yourself. The app will never do this for you.</p>

<h2 id="windowed">Running as a windowed app</h2>

<p>By default zettel runs as a local web server and you access it through a browser. If you'd prefer a real native window with no browser chrome — looking and behaving like a normal Mac application — there are two options.</p>

<p><strong>The lazy option (zero install):</strong> with the server running normally, open <code>http://127.0.0.1:5005</code> in Chrome or Edge, click the menu, and choose <em>Install page as app</em> (or in Safari: File → Add to Dock). You get a windowed app immediately, with a dock icon and everything. The catch is the server still has to be running in a terminal in the background.</p>

<p><strong>The proper option (one-time setup):</strong> install pywebview and use the <code>desktop.py</code> launcher, which bundles the server and the window into one process:</p>

<pre><code>pip install -r requirements-desktop.txt
python desktop.py</code></pre>

<p>This opens zettel in a native window backed by WebKit (the same engine Safari uses). The server starts in the background automatically and shuts down cleanly when you close the window. Same URLs, same notes, same bibliography integration — just no browser. The pywebview dependency is heavier than the base install (it pulls in pyobjc, around 50MB) but it's well-maintained and "just works" on macOS.</p>

<h2 id="exporting">Exporting to Word, LaTeX, HTML, and other formats</h2>

<p>The Zettelkasten is not just a thinking tool — it is also the upstream of your actual writing output. Any single note, any thread, or any group of notes sharing a tag can be exported to a finished document via <a href="https://pandoc.org">Pandoc</a>.</p>

<p><strong>Prerequisite:</strong> install Pandoc on your system. On macOS, <code>brew install pandoc</code>; on Ubuntu/Debian, <code>sudo apt install pandoc</code>; on Windows, see <a href="https://pandoc.org/installing.html">pandoc.org/installing</a>. If Pandoc is not installed, the export pages show install instructions instead of the export form.</p>

<p><strong>Three export sources, accessible from three places:</strong></p>

<ul>
  <li>From any note's view page, click <em>export</em> in the action row at the bottom — exports just that note.</li>
  <li>From a thread page (e.g. <code>/thread/dissonance-arc</code>), click <em>export this thread</em> in the header — exports the full chain in order, with each note as a section of the resulting document.</li>
  <li>From a tag page (e.g. <code>/tag/sts-theory</code>), click <em>export all</em> — exports every note carrying that tag.</li>
</ul>

<p>The export form lets you pick the output format (Word, LaTeX, HTML, EPUB, ODT, plain text, or pure Markdown) and toggle whether <code>@citekey</code> references should be processed by pandoc-citeproc using your configured BibTeX file (which produces formatted citations and an automatically-generated references section). The result downloads as a file you can open in Word, edit further, and turn into a paper.</p>

<p><strong>For PDF output</strong>, the easiest path is to export to HTML or Word and use your browser or word processor's print-to-PDF feature. Direct PDF export from Pandoc requires a LaTeX engine (XeLaTeX, pdfLaTeX, etc.); install MacTeX or TeX Live if you want this.</p>

<h2 id="stats">Writing stats</h2>

<p>The <a href="{{ url_for('stats_page') }}">stats page</a> shows total notes, total words, mean and median words per note, a 30-day notes-per-day sparkline, an hour-of-day distribution of when you write, your most-linked notes, and your top tags.</p>

<p>It is deliberately observation-only. There are no streaks, no daily targets, no "you didn't write today" notifications. Stats here are a way to see your work as a body across time, not a way to discipline yourself. Some weeks you will write a lot of notes; some weeks you will spend reading and thinking and write nothing. Both are correct. The stats page reflects this without judgment.</p>

<h2 id="sync">Sync and backup</h2>

<p>The corpus is a folder full of plain text files. Every backup tool ever invented works on it: Time Machine, rsync, Git, Dropbox, iCloud Drive, Syncthing. Two recommendations in particular.</p>

<h3>Backup: use Git for version history</h3>

<p>In the project folder, run:</p>

<pre><code>git init
git add notes/
git commit -m "initial commit"</code></pre>

<p>Then commit periodically — daily, weekly, whatever rhythm you like. You get free undo for everything you've ever written. To see the history of any single note:</p>

<pre><code>git log -p notes/20260407164313-mynote.md</code></pre>

<p>This is independent of the soft-delete trash and works even if the whole <code>notes/</code> folder gets accidentally destroyed. For convenience, you can wire up a cron job or a launchd plist to auto-commit nightly.</p>

<h3>Sync: use Syncthing for multi-device access</h3>

<p>Syncthing is a free, open source, end-to-end encrypted, peer-to-peer file sync tool. It mirrors a folder between two or more devices without any central server. The architecture of zettel is local-first: each device runs its own copy of the app pointing at its own copy of <code>notes/</code>, and Syncthing keeps the <code>notes/</code> folders in agreement.</p>

<p><strong>Critical rule:</strong> sync only the <code>notes/</code> folder. Never sync <code>zettel.db</code> — the database is per-machine and rebuilds itself from the notes; sharing it would corrupt it.</p>

<p><strong>Step 1 — Install on your Mac:</strong></p>

<pre><code>brew install syncthing
brew services start syncthing</code></pre>

<p>Open <code>http://127.0.0.1:8384</code> in any browser. You should see the Syncthing GUI. This is your local Syncthing's control panel.</p>

<p><strong>Step 2 — Install on your VPS:</strong> SSH into the VPS and run:</p>

<pre><code>sudo apt update
sudo apt install -y syncthing
sudo loginctl enable-linger $USER
systemctl --user enable syncthing.service
systemctl --user start syncthing.service</code></pre>

<p>The <code>enable-linger</code> command lets the service keep running after you log out of SSH. The Syncthing GUI on the VPS only listens on localhost for security, so to access it from your Mac, open an SSH tunnel:</p>

<pre><code>ssh -L 8384:127.0.0.1:8384 your_user@your.vps.example.com</code></pre>

<p>While that SSH session is open, visit <code>http://127.0.0.1:8384</code> on your Mac — you're now talking to the VPS's Syncthing GUI through the tunnel. (If your Mac's own Syncthing is also using port 8384, use a different local port: <code>ssh -L 8385:127.0.0.1:8384 ...</code> and visit <code>:8385</code>.)</p>

<p><strong>Step 3 — Pair the devices.</strong> Each Syncthing instance has a unique Device ID (a long string like <code>A1B2C3D-...</code>).</p>

<ol>
  <li>On the Mac GUI: click <em>Actions → Show ID</em> and copy the device ID.</li>
  <li>On the VPS GUI (via tunnel): click <em>Add Remote Device</em>, paste the Mac's ID, give it a name like "Mac Mini", and save.</li>
  <li>Back on the Mac GUI: a notification appears asking whether to add the VPS as a remote device. Accept it.</li>
</ol>

<p><strong>Step 4 — Share the notes folder:</strong></p>

<ol>
  <li>On the Mac GUI: click <em>Add Folder</em>. Set <em>Folder Path</em> to the absolute path of your zettel <code>notes/</code> directory (e.g. <code>/Users/you/zettel/notes</code>). Give it a label like "zettel-notes". In the <em>Sharing</em> tab, tick the box next to your VPS device. In the <em>File Versioning</em> tab, choose <em>Staggered File Versioning</em> with default settings. Save.</li>
  <li>On the VPS GUI: a notification appears asking whether to accept the new folder share. Click <em>Add</em>, then set the folder path to wherever you want zettel's notes to live on the VPS. Save.</li>
</ol>

<p>Within a minute, every <code>.md</code> file from your Mac will appear on the VPS, and the watcher on each side will pick up changes automatically. Conflict files (if two devices edit the same note simultaneously) are saved as <code>.sync-conflict-...</code> files alongside the original — nothing is ever silently lost.</p>

<p><strong>Optional: sync your bibliography too.</strong> If you use the Zotero/BibTeX integration, put the <code>.bib</code> file inside the same Syncthing folder so it travels with your notes.</p>

<h2 id="trouble">Troubleshooting</h2>

<dl>

<dt>The server won't start: "Address already in use"</dt>
<dd>Something else is on port 5005. Run with a different port: <code>ZETTEL_PORT=5006 python app.py</code> and visit <code>http://127.0.0.1:5006</code> instead.</dd>

<dt>I edited a file in vim/Obsidian and the web UI doesn't show my changes</dt>
<dd>Reload the page. The watcher updates the index in the background but the page itself doesn't auto-refresh. If a reload doesn't show the change, check that you saved the file directly inside <code>notes/</code> and not in a subdirectory.</dd>

<dt>Search returns no results for words I know are in my notes</dt>
<dd>The index may be out of sync. Stop the server (<kbd>Ctrl+C</kbd>), delete <code>zettel.db</code>, and restart. The watcher does a full reconciliation on startup and rebuilds the index from the <code>notes/</code> folder. This is always safe — the database is disposable.</dd>

<dt>A wikilink shows as broken even though the note exists</dt>
<dd>The link must use the destination note's exact 14-digit ID, not its title or filename. Find the destination note, look at its URL or the small <code>id</code> shown under its title, and use that exact number.</dd>

<dt>The citation picker doesn't appear when I type @</dt>
<dd>You don't have a bibliography configured. Set <code>ZETTEL_BIB_PATH</code> in your environment when starting the server and point it at a .bib file. See <a href="#cite">Citations</a> above.</dd>

<dt>I want to start completely fresh</dt>
<dd>Stop the server. Delete <code>zettel.db</code> and (optionally) move <code>notes/</code> aside. Restart. You'll get an empty Zettelkasten.</dd>

<dt>Something is broken in a way not covered here</dt>
<dd>Check the terminal where the server is running. Most errors print useful information there. Stop and restart the server — many transient problems clear up.</dd>

</dl>

